<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Blue Horde</h1><p class="st-meta">1242 CE – 1379 CE (approx.) · capital Sarai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western wing of the Golden Horde, the realm of Batu and his heirs on the Volga, Don and Dnieper steppes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the conquest of the western steppe and Rus' between 1236 and 1242, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochid</a> lands were divided into two wings. The western or right wing, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, is often called the Blue Horde, though medieval sources use the colour names inconsistently.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The western wing was the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, controlling the Rus' principalities, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> trade and the Crimean ports. Its khans, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a>, held authority over the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochid</a> realm. Under its rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among the elite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> grew into a major trading city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg's</a> murder in 1357, decades of civil war followed, and between 1378 and 1380 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/white-horde">White Horde</a> khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and reunited the two wings. The western lands later broke into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/great-horde">Great Horde</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/blue-horde.jpg" alt="Blue Horde" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Afil · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Horde_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1242 CE – 1379 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sarai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Batu Khan, Berke, Jani Beg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism, later Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kipchak Turkic, Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia, Ukraine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ulus of Batu, Western wing of the Golden Horde</dd></dl></aside></div>

